Expected Outcomes
Working SystemTap scripts capable of tracing file I/O, system calls, and latency in real time on a live CentOS/RHEL system
A reproducible method for generating synthetic I/O load and correlating it with SystemTap-reported bottlenecks
A self-refreshing, multi-metric performance dashboard combining CPU, syscall, and I/O statistics into one view
Troubleshooting
If stap fails with errors referencing missing debuginfo or mismatched kernel versions, what does uname -r report versus what rpm -qa | grep kernel-debuginfo shows, and how would you reconcile the two?
If a script compiles and runs but every counter stays at zero even while you generate load in another terminal, what does that suggest about the target() filter, the process you are generating load from, or whether the probe points you chose actually fire for the syscalls involved?
Conclusion
In this lab you installed and validated SystemTap on a live kernel, then built a progression of scripts that moved from simple system call counting to latency histograms, error tracking, synthetic load generation, and a combined real-time dashboard. Along the way you learned how to filter probes by process, aggregate statistics into global associative arrays, use SystemTap's built-in statistical aggregates such as @avg, @min, @max, and @hist_log, and reset counters on a timer to produce a rolling view of system behavior. You also practiced correlating a controlled I/O workload with the metrics your scripts reported, which is the same workflow used in production kernel-level troubleshooting. As a next step, explore SystemTap's tapset library to reuse higher-level abstractions instead of raw probes, experiment with stap -x <pid> to scope monitoring to a single running process, and compare your findings against perf or bpftrace to understand the tradeoffs between the different Linux tracing frameworks.
